  "prediction": "The report provides an overview of the federal budget process, focusing on the President's budgetary proposals and the Congressional Budget Office's (CBO) updated baseline budget projections. The President's budget outlines the administration's spending priorities for the upcoming fiscal year. The CBO's budget projections span 11 fiscal years and include separate chapters on the economic outlook, outlays, and receipts.\n\nThe report also mentions that printed copies of budget documents are available for purchase from the Government Publishing Office (GPO) and that the Congressional Research Service (CRS) has developed resources for Members of Congress and their staffs on appropriations and the federal budget process. These resources include explanations of budget concepts, terminology, and the congressional and executive budget process, among others.\n\nAdditionally, the report notes that the public can access Congress.gov, the legislative website produced by the Library of Congress, for information on the status of appropriations legislation. The site includes links to bills, committee and conference reports, and votes for the 12 regular, and any supplemental, appropriations bills.\n\nIn summary, the report outlines the President's budget process, the role of the CBO in providing updated budget projections, and various resources for the public and Congress to access information on the federal budget and appropriations process.",
  "scored_prediction": "The report provides an overview of the federal budget process, focusing on the President's budgetary proposals and the Congressional Budget Office's (CBO) updated baseline budget projections. The President's budget outlines the administration's spending priorities for the upcoming fiscal year. The CBO's budget projections span 11 fiscal years and include separate chapters on the economic outlook, outlays, and receipts.\n\nThe report also mentions that printed copies of budget documents are available for purchase from the Government Publishing Office (GPO) and that the Congressional Research Service (CRS) has developed resources for Members of Congress and their staffs on appropriations and the federal budget process. These resources include explanations of budget concepts, terminology, and the congressional and executive budget process, among others.\n\nAdditionally, the report notes that the public can access Congress.gov, the legislative website produced by the Library of Congress, for information on the status of appropriations legislation. The site includes links to bills, committee and conference reports, and votes for the 12 regular, and any supplemental, appropriations bills.\n\nIn summary, the report outlines the President's budget process, the role of the CBO in providing updated budget projections, and various resources for the public and Congress to access information on the federal budget and appropriations process.",
  "score": 27.802690083050134,
  "answers": [
    "Every year the President submits a series of volumes to Congress containing the President's proposed budget for the coming fiscal year. The President's submission is required on or after the first Monday in January, but no later than the first Monday in February (31 U.S.C. \u00a71105(a)). This year the President released the budget submission, in two installments, on March 11, and March 18, 2019. This report provides brief descriptions of the FY2020 budget volumes and related documents, together with internet addresses, Government Publishing Office (GPO) stock numbers, and prices for obtaining print copies of these publications. It also explains how to find the locations of government depository libraries, which can provide both printed copies for reference use and internet access to the online versions. This report will be updated as events warrant. Please note that neither the Congressional Research Service (CRS) nor the Library of Congress (LOC) distributes print copies of the budget documents."
  ],
